  <data key="d4">CORROBORATES</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.95</data>
</edge>
<edge source="884ef424-e8e1-4bf5-bf91-395b93140734" target="b5736d7a-37c0-4f1a-8fc9-f872a4494202">
  <data key="d3">The existence of a suicide clause limiting payouts within the first two years reinforces the conditions under which the death benefit from the ROP policy may not be paid, even though it otherwise provides substantial financial protection.</data>
  <data key="d4">EXCLUDES_SUICIDE</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.95</data>
</edge>
<edge source="884ef424-e8e1-4bf5-bf91-395b93140734" target="ea5bc4dd-d91e-4ff2-90de-0cc747100f5f">
  <data key="d3">The existence of a Return of Premium clause depends on the policy remaining valid throughout the term, which can be jeopardized if material misrepresentations were made at application—potentially leading to denial of the premium refund even if the insured survives.</data>
  <data key="d4">DEPENDS_ON</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.87</data>
</edge>
<edge source="884ef424-e8e1-4bf5-bf91-395b93140734" target="71fd7901-e2e8-4c64-a67d-555c1e72b157">
  <data key="d3">Fact Sentence 1 establishes the core nature of the policy as an ROP term plan, while Fact Sentence 2 confirms it lacks hazardous activity exclusions, supporting broader eligibility and consistent underwriting standards across the policy’s scope.</data>
  <data key="d4">COMPLIMENTS</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.85</data>
</edge>
<edge source="884ef424-e8e1-4bf5-bf91-395b93140734" target="75bca4d7-1be0-4be8-b8fb-1cf86a7af494">
  <data key="d3">Fact Sentence 1 describes a Return of Premium term life policy with broad coverage terms, while Fact Sentence 2 explicitly states there are no aviation exclusions, reinforcing that the policy covers deaths from aviation-related incidents without restriction.</data>
  <data key="d4">CORROBORATES</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.95</data>
</edge>
<edge source="884ef424-e8e1-4bf5-bf91-395b93140734" target="6e225e7e-3f3a-47fa-81d2-fc2a41f25361">
  <data key="d3">Fact_sentence_1 describes the core nature of the ROP policy, which includes returning all premiums upon surviving the term. Fact_sentence_2 expands on this by specifying the exact methods available to access those returns—through either surrender with deductions or waiting until term completion—thus clarifying implementation details tied to the promise made in fact_sentence_1.</data>
  <data key="d4">DEPENDS_ON</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.95</data>
</edge>
<edge source="884ef424-e8e1-4bf5-bf91-395b93140734" target="3cb7f968-8a8c-43f6-8023-1df17e94f6da">
  <data key="d3">The existence of a return-of-premium feature depends on continuous premium payments over the term; if the policy lapses, reinstatement becomes necessary to preserve eligibility for the refund, making this condition relevant to fulfilling the terms outlined in fact_sentence_1.</data>
  <data key="d4">DEPENDS_ON</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.85</data>
</edge>
<edge source="884ef424-e8e1-4bf5-bf91-395b93140734" target="562d8dc1-7081-489f-9ac5-c3774de62cee">
  <data key="d3">The first fact outlines key terms of an ROP policy, while the second explains what's required to revive it after lapsing. Together, they highlight aspects of policy continuity and eligibility beyond initial enrollment.</data>
  <data key="d4">DEPENDS_ON</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.75</data>
</edge>
<edge source="884ef424-e8e1-4bf5-bf91-395b93140734" target="976d21fc-a08c-4c4a-994c-c5ac61731c42">
  <data key="d3">The ROP policy's structure allows for premium refunds under normal circumstances, but if it lapses, reinstatement requires paying past-due premiums with interest, altering the path to recovery of those funds.</data>
  <data key="d4">DEPENDS_ON</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.75</data>
</edge>
<edge source="884ef424-e8e1-4bf5-bf91-395b93140734" target="6c3f8840-4647-406b-9929-e1b67417fe7f">
  <data key="d3">The existence of a Return of Premium feature depends on the insured surviving the term, but reinstatement after a lapse also requires the individual to remain alive—an essential precondition ensuring continuity of coverage and eventual access to refunded premiums.</data>
  <data key="d4">DEPENDS_ON</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.85</data>
</edge>
<edge source="884ef424-e8e1-4bf5-bf91-395b93140734" target="c1512491-2648-4091-bf70-f69ea67b0895">
  <data key="d3">The ROP benefit's final amount depends on whether the policy was reinstated, as reinstatement excludes the lapsed period from the calculation, potentially reducing the total refundable premiums.</data>
  <data key="d4">DEPENDS_ON</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.85</data>
</edge>
<edge source="884ef424-e8e1-4bf5-bf91-395b93140734" target="06dca34d-1332-47ca-87cb-7fe487dab3a4">
  <data key="d3">The existence of a Return of Premium feature depends on surviving the term without triggering exclusions like suicide within two years—a timeline reinitialized upon policy reinstatement.</data>
  <data key="d4">DEPENDS_ON</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.92</data>
</edge>
<edge source="884ef424-e8e1-4bf5-bf91-395b93140734" target="b4a16075-b6bb-4b9d-8614-8a4e078e9175">
  <data key="d3">The existence of a Return of Premium (ROP) feature in the policy justifies its higher cost compared to standard term life insurance, making the elevated price a direct consequence of the promised premium refund at maturity.</data>
  <data key="d4">DEPENDS_ON</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.95</data>
</edge>
