<edge source="834d28a5-622d-4fec-8a75-c824a028663b" target="80b3a0f3-2ae7-4269-ae27-bcba28448f8f">
  <data key="d3">The requirement to submit a certified death certificate supports verifying the insured's passing, while submitting a government-issued ID confirms the identity of the claiming beneficiary. Both are necessary but independent components of the claims submission.</data>
  <data key="d4">COMPLEMENTARY</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.85</data>
</edge>
<edge source="834d28a5-622d-4fec-8a75-c824a028663b" target="75780d21-da26-475c-b202-9491455a9f36">
  <data key="d3">Submitting a certified death certificate is necessary for processing a death benefit claim, whereas the $32,400 return is only applicable if the policyholder lives through the entire term and no death claim is filed.</data>
  <data key="d4">CONDITIONAL_RELATIONSHIP</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.3</data>
</edge>
<edge source="834d28a5-622d-4fec-8a75-c824a028663b" target="3cd53960-22e5-4307-8561-6aa1ffcfc09a">
  <data key="d3">The need to submit a certified death certificate ensures proper verification of the insured's demise, validating whether the $1,620 annual premium payments qualify for a death benefit payout.</data>
  <data key="d4">REQUIRED_FOR</data>
  <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
  <data key="d5">0.85</data>
</edge>
